tgs:Work.ThinkingLikeAMountain a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thinking Like a Mountain: Towards a Council of All Beings"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1988 book (New Society Publishers), co-authored by John Seed, Joanna Macy, Arne Naess, and Pat Fleming — describes the Council of All Beings ritual and the philosophical/experiential foundations of deep ecology. Translated into 12+ languages. Directly discussed in RR-17 as the founding text of the Work That Reconnects lineage."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnSeed ;
    dct:issued "1988"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via RR-13's own real linked show notes.
#############################################

tgs:Work.FutureCouncilFilm a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Future Council"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2024 documentary film directed by Damon Gameau, following eight children (ages 11-14) across Europe by biodiesel-fueled bus, confronting corporate leaders (Nestlé, Decathlon, ING, among others) about climate accountability. Features an animated antagonist character, 'Groth,' explicitly based on Nate Hagens' own real 'Economic Superorganism' framing (tgs:Concept.Superorganism) — a direct, real instance of this graph's own foundational concept being adapted into mainstream cultural media. Premiered Melbourne International Film Festival (Aug 2024); later screened at the UN General Assembly; theatrical release planned 2025 across Australia, Europe, and America."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.DamonGameau ;
    dct:issued "2024"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via a real Hylo community post
# (MJSullivan's own "Great Simplification" group thread), verified
# independently before minting. Real sequel to Work.HospicingModernity
# if that exists — check name below before assuming.
#############################################

tgs:Work.OutgrowingModernity a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Outgrowing Modernity: Navigating Complexity, Complicity, and Collapse with Accountability and Compassion"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2025 book by Vanessa Machado de Oliveira (Andreotti), published August 12, 2025 — the real sequel to her 2021 'Hospicing Modernity,' with contributions from Keri Facer and Awo Fatokun Faniyii. Explores 'neurocolonization' — modernity's effects on how people think, act, imagine, and adapt — and includes real documented material on the author's own collaborative work with AI systems (a recurring character, 'Aiden'). Co-founder credit: Gesturing Towards Decolonial Futures (GTDF) Arts/Research Collective."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.VanessaAndreotti ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.amazon.com/Outgrowing-Modernity-Navigating-Complexity-Accountability/dp/B0DMVDJ4VG> ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — resolves a real, long-standing PENDING item
# flagged since the very start of this project ("Scenario Thinking"
# book citation), surfaced via Frankly_139's own real show notes.
#############################################

tgs:Work.ScenarioThinkingHistoricalEvolution a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Scenario Thinking: A Historical Evolution of Strategic Foresight"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2018 academic work (Cambridge University Press, 'Elements in Business Strategy' series) by R. Bradley MacKay (University of St Andrews) and Peter McKiernan (University of Strathclyde). Traces scenario thinking's roots to ancient civilizations (including Mesopotamian and Assyrian foresight practices) through the French and Anglo-American schools of modern scenario planning, arguing scenario thinking should be understood as distinct from and more fundamental than scenario planning as a managerial technique."@en ;
    dct:creator "R. Bradley MacKay"@en, "Peter McKiernan"@en ;
    dct:issued "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108571494> ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via TGS_167's own real linked show
# notes and directly discussed at length in the transcript itself.
#############################################

tgs:Work.WildEdgeOfSorrow a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Wild Edge of Sorrow: Rituals of Renewal and the Sacred Work of Grief"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Bestselling book by Francis Weller (North Atlantic Books), the real, direct source of the 'Five Gates of Grief' framework discussed at length in TGS_167. Real endorsement from Anderson Cooper ('One of the best books on grief I have ever read') and from Joanna Macy, already discussed extensively in this graph's own RR-17 build."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.FrancisWeller ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.francisweller.net/books.html> ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via TGS_213's own real linked show
# notes and directly discussed at length in the transcript itself.
#############################################

tgs:Work.TheArrogantApe a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Arrogant Ape: The Myth of Human Exceptionalism and Why It Matters"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2025 book by Christine Webb (Penguin Random House) — a real New York Times Notable Book of 2025, longlisted for the Nayef Al-Rodhan Book Prize. Argues human exceptionalism is an ideology relying more on human culture than biology, more on delusion and faith than evidence, drawing on Webb's own primatology research."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ChristineWebb ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.penguinrandomhouse.com/books/717436/the-arrogant-ape-by-christine-webb/> ;
    .

#############################################
# ADDED 2026-08-13 — surfaced via TGS_138's own real linked show
# notes and directly discussed at length in the transcript itself.
#############################################

tgs:Work.TaoOfPhysics a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Tao of Physics: An Exploration of the Parallels Between Modern Physics and Eastern Mysticism"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "1975 book by Fritjof Capra, his real first and best-known work — still in print in 40+ editions, referenced alongside a statue of Shiva in the courtyard of CERN. Argues for real parallels between quantum theory/relativity and Eastern mystical traditions (Hinduism, Buddhism, Taoism), directly inspired by his own real undergraduate reading of Werner Heisenberg's 'Physics and Philosophy.'"@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.FritjofCapra ;
    dct:issued "1975"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <https://www.fritjofcapra.net/about/> ;
    .

# added 2026-08-25

tgs:Work.MaterialWorld a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Material World: The Six Raw Materials That Shape Modern Civilization"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "2023 book by Ed Conway (Penguin Random House / WH Allen). An Economist and Sunday Times Book of the Year, shortlisted for the 2023 FT Business Book of the Year Award. Traces the full extraction-to-product supply chain of six materials — sand, salt, iron, copper, oil, and lithium — arguing these physically underpin the modern economy far out of proportion to their small direct share of GDP."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.EdConway ;
    dct:issued "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    owl:sameAs <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Material_World_(book)> ;
    .

# added 2026-08-29

tgs:Work.DeseretFarmerBailout2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "There's no bailout for the American Farmer"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Deseret Magazine, March 2026. Documents structural farm consolidation via 'get big or get out' market logic — 63 farms lost per day, 162,000 farms lost 2017-2024, 24 million acres lost. Reports that even government bailouts have failed to reverse the trend. Real, direct thematic overlap with TGS_198's own discussion of concentrated economic power squeezing out smaller, locally-owned businesses."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.deseret.com/magazine/2026/03/07/america-family-farms-in-decline/> ;
    dct:issued "2026-03"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.WorldBankFoodSecurityUpdate2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Food Security Update"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "World Bank, June 2026. Authoritative, non-partisan monitoring brief documenting real global fertilizer-price pressure (35% increase) and food-supply fragility. Descriptive rather than prescriptive — doesn't itself argue for local food systems as the remedy. Real, direct connection to TGS_198's own discussion of supply-chain fragility (the single-slaughterhouse and baby-formula-plant examples)."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.worldbank.org/en/topic/foodsecurity> ;
    dct:issued "2026-06"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.PewDecliningTrust2025 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Americans' Declining Trust in Each Other and Reasons Behind It"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Pew Research Center, May 2025. Documents declining interpersonal trust connected to political polarization, including spatial/social sorting that reduces meaningful cross-party contact. Descriptive survey data, not prescriptive. TGS_198 supplies a real causal mechanism this piece's data doesn't on its own — Stacy Mitchell's own direct argument connecting economic powerlessness to democratic erosion."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2025/05/08/americans-declining-trust-in-each-other-and-reasons-behind-it/> ;
    dct:issued "2025-05"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.CarbonMajorsWaterCrisis2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Carbon emissions exacerbating the Western US water crisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Williams, E.L., Abatzoglou, J.T., Phillips, C.A., Safeeq, M., Sadai, S., Chegwidden, O.S., Fernandez-Bou, A.S., Merner, L.D., Ortiz-Partida, J.P. Communications Earth & Environment 7, 675 (2026), published by Nature Portfolio, Union of Concerned Scientists and UC Merced. First attribution study of its kind: finds 40-64% of climate-driven western US water crisis impacts (1950-2024) — snowpack loss, streamflow decline, irrigation demand increase — attributable to 122 'Carbon Majors' (fossil fuel and cement companies), including roughly one-third of California Central Valley's climate-related groundwater decline. Open Access, CC-BY 4.0 — direct quotation with attribution explicitly permitted. Source article for WWNS #1 ('The 122 Companies Behind the Western Water Crisis')."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.nature.com/articles/s43247-026-03900-6> ;
    dct:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode> ;
    dct:issued "2026-08-25"^^xsd:date ;
    .

tgs:Work.Ultrasocial2021 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ultrasocial: The Evolution of Human Nature and the Quest for a Sustainable Future"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "John Gowdy, Cambridge University Press, October 2021. Argues human 'ultrasociality' — the same evolutionary category as ants, termites, and other eusocial species — emerged from the economic surplus dynamics of agriculture, not from a fixed 'selfish gene' human nature. Co-authored research on ants/social insects with Lisi Krall (SUNY Cortland, not yet built in this graph) underlies much of the book's argument. Directly cited in TGS_14's own real show notes."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnGowdy ;
    dct:source <https://www.amazon.com/Ultrasocial-Evolution-Nature-Sustainable-Future/dp/110883826X> ;
    dct:issued "2021-10"^^xsd:gYearMonth ;
    .

tgs:Work.PsychopathicSelectionHypothesisBook a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Psychopathic Selection Hypothesis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Greg Elliott's own book, self-described in the TGS_231 transcript as published 'back in May' (year not independently confirmed — the episode's own real, wider context suggests 2026, consistent with dct:issued below, but not verified via direct fetch). Argues dark triad traits (psychopathy, Machiavellianism, narcissism) are selected for at an institutional/civilizational scale via game-theoretic dynamics, accelerating with the agricultural revolution and again with fossil fuel expansion. Endorsed by William Rees (already a real Persona in this graph, RR-2) — the two are described in the transcript as having 'become pretty close friends' as a result."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.GregElliott ;
    .

tgs:Work.BrokenMoney2023 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Broken Money: Why Our Financial System is Failing Us and How We Can Make it Better"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Lyn Alden, 2023 best-selling book on the past, present, and future of money through the lens of technology and energy. Directly cited in her own TGS_113 appearance as her most recent major work."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.LynAlden ;
    dct:source <https://www.lynalden.com/broken-money/> ;
    dct:issued "2023"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.ZombiesInWesternCulture a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Zombies in Western Culture: A 21st Century Crisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "John Vervaeke and Christopher Mastropietro (co-author, not yet built in this graph). Integrates cognitive science and psychology to address the meaning crisis in Western society, using the cultural zombie-figure as a real, structured metaphor for the modal confusion Vervaeke describes in his own TGS_158 appearance — 'we're not starving calorically, but we're starving nutritionally.'"@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JohnVervaeke ;
    dct:source <https://johnvervaeke.com/books/zombies-in-western-culture-a-twenty-first-century-crisis/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheseWildsBeyondOurFences a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "These Wilds Beyond Our Fences: Letters to My Daughter on Humanity's Search for Home"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Bayo Akomolafe, North Atlantic Books, 2017."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.BayoAkomolafe ;
    dct:issued "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.PewAmericansMorallyBad2026 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "In 25-Country Survey, Americans Especially Likely To View Fellow Citizens as Morally Bad"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Jonathan Evans, Kirsten Lesage, Manolo Corichi, William Miner, Andrew Prozorovsky, Julia Armeli, Alan Cooperman. Pew Research Center, March 5, 2026. Real, striking finding: the US is the only one of 25 countries surveyed where more adults rate their fellow citizens' morality as bad (53%) than good (47%). Notably, this isn't explained by Americans being more morally strict in general — on most of nine specific moral-judgment questions, the US sits mid-pack internationally. A real, direct partisan mechanism is identified instead: Democrats/leaners are far more likely than Republicans/leaners to rate fellow Americans as immoral (60% vs. 46%). Real methodology: 25-country Global Attitudes Survey, Jan-May 2025 (US n=3,605, Mar 24-30, 2025, plus a separate ATP survey of 8,937 US adults, May 5-11, 2025)."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.pewresearch.org/religion/2026/03/05/in-25-country-survey-americans-especially-likely-to-view-fellow-citizens-as-morally-bad/> ;
    dct:issued "2026-03-05"^^xsd:date ;
    .

tgs:Work.LePlanDeTransformation a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Plan de transformation de l'économie française (The Plan to Transform the French Economy)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The Shift Project, led by Jean-Marc Jancovici, begun during COVID. A real, distinctive methodology: reorganizes the economy around physical flows (materials, energy, employment by sector) rather than monetary figures, modeling what a 5%-per-year greenhouse gas reduction actually requires sector by sector. Published as a book; sold over 100,000 copies in France, a real, exceptional figure for a policy essay there."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JeanMarcJancovici ;
    dct:source <https://theshiftproject.org/en/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.LeMondeSansFin a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Le Monde sans fin (The World Without End)"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Graphic novel by Jean-Marc Jancovici and illustrator Christophe Blain (not yet built in this graph), 2022. Real, exceptional commercial success: the best-selling book in France in 2022, over 500,000 copies — a striking figure for a book about energy and climate."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JeanMarcJancovici ;
    dct:issued "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.GoliathsCurse a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Goliath's Curse: The History and Future of Societal Collapse"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Luke Kemp, Penguin, 2025 (US release Sep 23, 2025). Argues 'civilization' is a poorly-defined, historically loaded term better replaced with 'Goliath' — a society organized around dominance hierarchies enforced by violence, distinct from earlier, more egalitarian large-scale human settlements (real examples cited directly: the Indus Valley/Harappan civilization, early Teotihuacán) that lacked centralized rule. Traces a real, recurring pattern: societies beginning egalitarian, becoming progressively more unequal and hierarchical, then destabilizing and collapsing once inequality reaches a high level."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.LukeKemp ;
    dct:source <https://www.penguin.co.uk/books/321192/goliaths-curse-by-kemp-luke/9780241741238> ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.Disorder2022 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Disorder: Hard Times in the 21st Century"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Helen Thompson, Oxford University Press, 2022. Real, striking coincidence: the UK edition released the same day Russia invaded Ukraine. Argues the political shocks of the 2010s (Brexit, Trump's election) have structural, long-history explanations rooted in energy — the difficulties of the fossil fuel economy and the energy transition — rather than being standalone episodes of national political drama."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HelenThompson ;
    dct:source <https://global.oup.com/academic/product/disorder-9780198864981?cc=us&lang=en&> ;
    dct:issued "2022"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.OilAndTheWesternEconomicCrisis a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Oil and the Western Economic Crisis"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Helen Thompson, Springer, 2017. Her own real, direct account: at the time of publication, 'most people thought, well, why is she writing about that' — energy-aware political economy was not yet a widely legible frame; that changed substantially after Disorder's 2022 release."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HelenThompson ;
    dct:issued "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheStoryOfCO2 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Story of CO2 Is the Story of Everything: A Four-Billion-Year Chronicle of Planet Earth and Our Tumultuous Relationship with Carbon Dioxide"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Peter Brannen, Ecco, August 2025. Reframes CO2 from industrial pollutant to the substance fundamental to Earth's habitability itself — the 'control knob' for planetary temperature and ocean chemistry, and the ultimate source of all carbon-based life. Grew directly out of research for his earlier book, tgs:Work.TheEndsOfTheWorld: while auditing geochemistry courses, Brannen found most mass extinctions are linked to runaway CO2 injections or declines, not the asteroid-impact narrative most people assume."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PeterBrannen ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheEndsOfTheWorld a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ends of the World: Volcanic Apocalypses, Lethal Oceans, and Our Quest to Understand Earth's Past Mass Extinctions"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Peter Brannen, Ecco. Covers Earth's five major mass extinctions. Featured in Greta Thunberg's The Climate Book and the Best American Science and Nature Writing series. Subject of Brannen's real first TGS appearance, TGS_103."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PeterBrannen ;
    .

tgs:Work.TrueHuman a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "True Human: Reimagining Ourselves at the End of Our World"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Samantha Sweetwater, Q4 2025. Offers a path for sense-making through the metacrisis, framing it as a spiritual opportunity calling for cultivated relational intelligence and moral imagination, centered on a shift from the 'hero's journey' to what she calls the 'kinship journey' — recognizing one's own life as meaningful only in service to something larger than oneself."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SamanthaSweetwater ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.ThermalPowerAndClimateChange a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Thermal Power and Climate Change: A Data-Driven Analysis of Cause and Effect, 1800-2100"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tad Patzek, preprint available (published book forthcoming at time of TGS_225 recording). Also references a real, distinct research contribution discussed directly in that episode: a preprint under peer review at Geophysical Research Letters identifying a declining Earth albedo as an additional accelerant of global warming over the past 26 years, layered on top of accelerating ocean heat absorption and ice-sheet melt."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TadPatzek ;
    dct:source <https://eartharxiv.org/repository/view/10865/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.BitterHarvest a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bitter Harvest: An Inquiry into the War Between Economy and Earth"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Lisi Krall, SUNY Press. Traces the formation and evolution of the economic Superorganism from the cultivation of annual grains through to global capitalism, arguing capitalism is the direct legacy of the agricultural revolution rather than a distinct later rupture."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.LisiKrall ;
    dct:source <https://sunypress.edu/Books/B/Bitter-Harvest> ;
    .

tgs:Work.ForTheCommonGood a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "For the Common Good: Redirecting the Economy toward Community, the Environment, and a Sustainable Future"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Herman Daly (with theologian John B. Cobb Jr.), Beacon Press, 1989. Real, direct personal significance: alongside tgs:Work.ValuingTheEarth, one of the two Daly books Nate read that directly caused him to leave his Wall Street career."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HermanDaly ;
    dct:issued "1989"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.ValuingTheEarth a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Valuing the Earth: Economics, Ecology, Ethics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Herman Daly and Kenneth Townsend (eds.), MIT Press. Real, direct personal significance: the book Nate's own words credit as the main thing that propelled him to leave his Wall Street career roughly a year after reading it."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HermanDaly ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheMythOfTheMachine a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Myth of the Machine"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Lewis Mumford, two volumes (Technics and Human Development, 1967; The Pentagon of Power, 1970). Develops the real concept of the 'megamachine' — large hierarchical organizations using humans as component parts, exemplified across history from pyramid-building to modern nuclear-armed states — and distinguishes 'polytechnic' technology (serving diverse human needs) from 'monotechnic' technology (pursued for its own sake, at humanity's expense)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.LewisMumford ;
    dct:issued "1970"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheFutureIsRural a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Future Is Rural: Food System Adaptations to the Great Simplification"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Jason Bradford, Post Carbon Institute, 2019. Real, direct source for the show's own established phrase 'the future is rural' — already cited directly in Frankly_138."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JasonBradford ;
    dct:source <https://www.postcarbon.org/publications/the-future-is-rural/> ;
    dct:issued "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.BrettonWoodsIII a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Bretton Woods III"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Real theory popularized by Zoltan Pozsar (then an economist at Credit Suisse), proposing a new world monetary order centered on commodity-backed currencies in the East, weakening the existing Eurodollar system — following Bretton Woods I (dollar pegged to gold) and Bretton Woods II (the free-floating dollar after that peg's abandonment). Pozsar himself, not built as a Persona in this graph, has no direct connection to Nate or this show; this Work exists here specifically because Michael Every (already built) wrote a real, extensive published rebuttal of it."@en ;
    dct:source <https://coinmarketcap.com/academy/article/bretton-woods-iii-and-crypto> ;
    .

tgs:Work.HistoryForTomorrow a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "History for Tomorrow: Inspiration from the Past for the Future of Humanity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Roman Krznaric. Draws on historical precedent for societies that navigated existential crises, arguing history offers real, concrete inspiration for governance, community organization, and economic redesign rather than serving only as cautionary tales."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RomanKrznaric ;
    .

tgs:Work.ThePopulationBomb a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Population Bomb"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Paul Ehrlich, 1968. Real, foundational and widely-debated book on the risks of unchecked human population growth. Real, direct personal significance to this graph: Nate's own father read it."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PaulEhrlich ;
    dct:issued "1968"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheRighteousMind a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Righteous Mind: Why Good People Are Divided by Politics and Religion"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Jonathan Haidt, 2012. New York Times bestseller examining the intuitive foundations of morality and how moral reasoning varies across cultural and political divisions."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.JonathanHaidt ;
    dct:issued "2012"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.PratiquementDurable a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Pratiquement Durable: 2000 Watt Eco-Neighborhoods – A Model for a Sustainable Lifestyle Towards a Post-Oil Democratic Society"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Peter Strack. Currently only available in French. Advocates for 2000-watt-per-capita neighborhoods as a lower-energy, higher-connection alternative to typical Western consumption levels."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PeterStrack ;
    dct:source <https://pratiquement-durable.com/en/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.DopamineNation a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Dopamine Nation: Finding Balance in the Age of Indulgence"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Anna Lembke, New York Times bestseller. Explores how to moderate compulsive overconsumption in a dopamine-overloaded modern environment."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.AnnaLembke ;
    dct:source <https://www.annalembke.com/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.Amazonia4 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Amazonia 4.0"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Initiative developed by Carlos Nobre, demonstrating the feasibility of a new socio-bioeconomy built on standing forests and flowing rivers in the Amazon, as a real, direct economic alternative to deforestation-driven extraction."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CarlosNobre ;
    dct:source <https://amazonia4.org/> ;
    .

tgs:Work.AncientFutures a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ancient Futures: Learning from Ladakh"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Helena Norberg-Hodge. Real, direct account of tradition and change in Ladakh ('Little Tibet'), examining the ecological, social, and psychological effects of rapid economic globalization on a traditional culture. Translated into over 40 languages; over half a million copies sold. Also produced as a real, companion documentary film of the same title."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.HelenaNorbergHodge ;
    dct:source <https://www.helenanorberghodge.com/writing-films> ;
    .

tgs:Work.AgainstTheMachine a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Against the Machine: On the Unmaking of Humanity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Paul Kingsnorth. Real, direct historical overview of philosophers and thinkers who observed Superorganism-like qualities emerging at scale from human activity, framing 'the Machine' as a civilizational force that turns creatures into components. Structured largely around a real, central Wendell Berry quote on whether humans will choose to be creatures or machines."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.PaulKingsnorth ;
    .

tgs:Work.Ishmael a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Ishmael"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Daniel Quinn, 1992. A telepathic gorilla teaches a human student the real, distinctive 'Takers and Leavers' framework — civilizations that believe the world was made for humans to master and exploit ('Takers') versus cultures that see humans as one part of a larger living community ('Leavers'). Real, direct personal significance to this graph: Nate first dismissed the book after five pages, then read it cover to cover on September 11, 2001, when the markets were closed."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.DanielQuinn ;
    dct:issued "1992"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheAscentOfHumanity a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Ascent of Humanity"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Charles Eisenstein, 2007. His first major book, written in part as a direct response to Daniel Quinn, Derrick Jensen, and John Zerzan's anti-civilization discourse, which narrates human civilization as a fall from a prior state of harmony and connection."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CharlesEisenstein ;
    dct:issued "2007"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.SacredEconomics a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Sacred Economics"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Charles Eisenstein, 2011."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CharlesEisenstein ;
    dct:issued "2011"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheMoreBeautifulWorld a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The More Beautiful World Our Hearts Know Is Possible"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Charles Eisenstein, 2013."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CharlesEisenstein ;
    dct:issued "2013"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.ClimateANewStory a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Climate: A New Story"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Charles Eisenstein, 2018."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.CharlesEisenstein ;
    dct:issued "2018"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.BraidingSweetgrass a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Braiding Sweetgrass: Indigenous Wisdom, Scientific Knowledge, and the Teachings of Plants"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Robin Wall Kimmerer, 2013. On the interdependence of people and the plant world, drawing on both Indigenous knowledge and Western botanical science. Won the 2014 Sigurd F. Olson Nature Writing Award; sold over 2 million copies worldwide within a decade of publication."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.RobinWallKimmerer ;
    dct:issued "2013"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.IfAnyoneBuildsItEveryoneDies a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "If Anyone Builds It, Everyone Dies: Why Superhuman AI Would Kill Us All"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Nate Soares and Eliezer Yudkowsky, 2025. Real, direct argument that current AI development methods — 'growing' systems through large-scale training rather than 'crafting' them through direct, understood specification — make catastrophic, unintended outcomes from superintelligent AI a likely rather than remote risk."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.NateSoares, tgs:Human.EliezerYudkowsky ;
    dct:source <https://ifanyonebuildsit.com/> ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheMarketMyth a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Market Myth"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tomas Björkman, 2016. Real, direct critique noting that roughly 85% of exchange in today's markets involves immaterial property rights rather than physical goods."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TomasBjorkman ;
    dct:issued "2016"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheNordicSecret a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Nordic Secret"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tomas Björkman and Lene Rachel Andersen, 2017. Explores the real, historical Nordic Bildung educational tradition — folk high schools and adult-development retreat centers — as a possible model for cultivating civic maturity at scale."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TomasBjorkman ;
    dct:issued "2017"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheWorldWeCreate a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The World We Create: From God to Market"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Tomas Björkman, 2019."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.TomasBjorkman ;
    dct:issued "2019"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.DieWise a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Die Wise: A Manifesto for Sanity and Soul"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Stephen Jenkinson. Award-winning book drawing on his years as a palliative-care counselor, arguing modern Anglo-North American culture is death-phobic and grief-illiterate, and that genuine skillfulness in dying and grieving must be actively learned rather than assumed."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.StephenJenkinson ;
    dct:issued "2015"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.Matrimony a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "Matrimony: Ritual, Culture, and the Heart's Work"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Stephen Jenkinson, 2025. Discussed on his TGS appearance as a forthcoming release, then still weeks from publication. Examines marriage not primarily as a legal or romantic institution but as a real, alchemical cultural ritual — distinguishing a 'vow' (a present-moment commitment requiring no future) from a 'promise' (inherently future-reliant)."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.StephenJenkinson ;
    dct:issued "2025"^^xsd:gYear ;
    .

tgs:Work.TheWormAtTheCore a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Worm at the Core: On the Role of Death in Life"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Sheldon Solomon, Jeff Greenberg, and Tom Pyszczynski. Synthesizes roughly three decades of real, empirical Terror Management Theory research, examining how the uniquely human awareness of mortality shapes behavior, culture, politics, and religion."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SheldonSolomon ;
    .

tgs:Work.InTheWakeOf911 a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "In the Wake of 9/11: The Psychology of Terror"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Sheldon Solomon, Jeff Greenberg, and Tom Pyszczynski. Applies Terror Management Theory directly to the September 11 attacks and their real, documented psychological aftermath."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.SheldonSolomon ;
    .

tgs:Work.DenialOfDeath a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "The Denial of Death"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Ernest Becker. Won the 1974 Pulitzer Prize. Real, foundational text for Terror Management Theory, arguing much of human culture and behavior functions as a defense against the awareness of mortality."@en ;
    dct:creator tgs:Human.ErnestBecker ;
    .

tgs:Work.NewEconomyForTheBrazilianAmazon a thinkr:Work, owl:NamedIndividual ;
    skos:prefLabel "New Economy for the Brazilian Amazon"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Real research initiative on economic incentives and payment structures for standing-forest conservation in the Brazilian Amazon, directly discussed by André Guimarães."@en ;
    dct:source <https://www.wri.org/research/new-economy-brazil-amazon> ;
    .
